Hi Learnosity team,
We need your opinion on a plan to convert our Math Formula questions.
 
Scenario
 
A large part of our math content uses the formulaV2 question type — about 334,000 question  in production.
We have a long-standing accessibility issue with formulaV2: it is not fully accessible for screen-reader and assistive-technology users. Based on Learnosity's guidance, the accessible path is Cloze Math (clozeformula), which we already author and render in our platform today.

The problem is scale. We cannot manually reauthor hundreds of thousands of questions - the cost and time are not feasible. So we want to automatically convert existing formulaV2 questions into clozeformula.
 
From our analysis, the two JSON formats are very close. 

The main differences are:

the type value,
clozeformula needs the math wrapped in \( \) in the template (HTML) instead of pure LaTeX,
validation answers are nested per blank in clozeformula, while formulaV2 stores them in a flatter structure.
 
About 9% of our items are multi-blank questions where one combined answer string covers several blanks. These are the risky cases, because the single combined answer must be split into one answer per blank, or scoring could break. The remaining ~91% are single-blank or simple items that should convert cleanly.
 
Before touching live content, we want to confirm with you that our conversion approach is correct and safe for scoring and accessibility.
 
Solution
We plan to auto-convert formulaV2 → clozeformula, store converted JSON in a shadow field first, test behind a flag, then release only after scoring/render checks pass.


Example

Before (formulaV2):
{
  "type": "formulaV2",
  "is_math": true,
  "template": "\\sec(\\theta)=response,\\ \\csc(\\theta)=response",
  "ui_style": {
    "type": "block-on-focus-keyboard"
  },
  "validation": {
    "scoring_type": "exactMatch",
    "valid_response": {
      "score": 1,
      "value": [
        {
          "method": "equivSymbolic",
          "options": {
            "decimalPlaces": 10
          },
          "value": "\\sec(\\theta)=-\\frac{13}{5},\\ \\csc(\\theta)=\\frac{13}{12}"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}


After (clozeformula):
{
  "type": "clozeformula",
  "is_math": true,
  "math_renderer": "mathquill",
  "template": "\\(\\sec(\\theta)=\\)response, \\(\\csc(\\theta)=\\)response",
  "response_containers": [],
  "ui_style": {
    "type": "block-on-focus-keyboard"
  },
  "validation": {
    "scoring_type": "exactMatch",
    "valid_response": {
      "score": 1,
      "value": [
        [
          {
            "method": "equivSymbolic",
            "options": {
              "decimalPlaces": 10
            },
            "value": "-\\frac{13}{5}"
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "method": "equivSymbolic",
            "options": {
              "decimalPlaces": 10
            },
            "value": "\\frac{13}{12}"
          }
        ]
      ]
    }
  }
}


Questions for you

Is auto-converting formulaV2 → clozeformula recommended?
For multi-blank items, is splitting the combined answer per blank correct?
Any official converter, checklist, or known risks?

Happy to share more JSON samples.

Thanks,
Sulanjala Fonseka

Hello!

We have encountered an issue regarding when we set an item's tags in the author app via `setItemTags()`. We pass these tags in a certain order (ordered on our end), the array as we pass it looks something like this:

```
[
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-1"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-2"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-3"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-4"
    }
]
```

The user then saves the item with this new list of tags via the Author App "Save" button. However, when the user goes back to the item list or even edits the item again, the tags are now shown in a different order:

```
[
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-1"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-3"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-4"
    },
    {
        "type": "Standard",
        "name": "DOK-2"
    }
]
```

This is causing issues with the way we check if there are changes to the tags. In theory if we remove a tag, then add the same tag back, it should result in no net changes, and thus the "Save" button being disabled. However, because the tags are now in a different order, the Author App enables the "Save" button.

Ideally the order that the tags are in when we pass them to `setItemTags()` would be preserved, unless there's some default sort behavior under the hood that we need to account for.

I saw the TagsV2 object potentially has a `sort_key` metadata field (https://help.learnosity.com/hc/en-us/articles/360000758597-Understanding-Tag-Formats-for-Content-Creation-and-Filtering#:~:text=TagsV2%20object%20with%20additional%20Tag%20name%20metadata) but when I tried setting that based on the order we desire it didn't persist after saving the item.

Is this the expected behavior? Could you tell me what sort of sort order the tags are shuffled into on your end once the item is saved? Or is there something else we can do when setting the tags so the order persists once the item is saved?

Thanks, and please let me know if you have any questions or need any more information.

